  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nThe center of an upper low is very evident on satellite imagery over\neastern Kentucky early this morning. This low is forecast to continue\nmoving northeast, reaching the Pittsburgh area by 15Z. Along and\nnorth of the track of the low, widespread rain is expected to\ncontinue.\n\nThe forecast concern for the morning is regarding the potential for\nsome snow to mix in with the rain. There are two areas where this may\noccur. First, parts of the Kentucky bluegrass area have already\nobserved some light snow early this morning (including Lexington), \nwith temperatures around 34 degrees. Some light snow could mix in in\nthe far southern ILN CWA over the next few hours. Then, closer to\n12Z, thermal profiles will support a mix of rain and snow in the far\nwestern and northwestern ILN CWA. The HRRR has been a little more\naggressive than other models in suggesting some light accumulations\ncould occur, but most of the model guidance suggests forcing and\nprecipitation intensity will be significantly diminishing by the time\nsnow could start to mix in, suggesting just a light mix with no\naccumulations.\n\nThere will likely be a general lull in precipitation during the late\nmorning and early afternoon, as the upper low moves away. A secondary\ndisturbance will bring some additional rain during the late afternoon\nand evening, along with an increase in westerly winds (some gusts of\n20-30 knots will be possible). Precipitation is expected to end from\nwest to east during the 00Z-06Z time frame.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nThere will be a very brief period of mid level ridging early\nSaturday, before the flow across the Ohio Valley becomes more zonal\nheading into the rest of the weekend. By Sunday morning, a trough\nwill be in place over the middle of the CONUS, moving east into the\nOhio Valley by Sunday evening. Ahead of the mid-level trough, a\nsurface cold front (and likely a weak surface low embedded within)\nwill cross the area. This will bring widespread rain Sunday into\nSunday night, though amounts will generally be a half inch or less. A\ntight pressure gradient behind the front, along with the switch to\ncold advection, will also bring a period of wind gusts to 25-30\nknots.\n\nColder air is expected behind the cold front on Monday, but a gradual\nwarming trend is then expected through the rest of the week.\nConfidence is low on the next chance for precipitation, but it will\nprobably be late in the week if something moves in.",
